  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a scanning tunneling microscopy experiment on a layered graphene device. A sharp STM tip hovers above a bilayer graphene sheet, while the graphene sits on a boron nitride insulating layer and a graphite base that acts as a gate electrode. A gate voltage is applied between the graphite and the graphene stack, indicating that the electronic state of the bilayer graphene can be tuned electrically during measurement.\n\nThe main visual message is that the STM probes a small local region of bilayer graphene while the gate controls its carrier density or energy landscape from below. Several small red dots with circular and upward arrows are drawn across the graphene surface, suggesting localized electronic or spin/valley-like states distributed over the sheet. One highlighted circular region directly beneath the STM tip emphasizes the local area being measured.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates the combination of local STM access with global electrostatic gating in a clean van der Waals heterostructure. It conveys how bilayer graphene on boron nitride over graphite provides a controlled platform for studying tunable microscopic electronic behavior, though the exact meaning of the arrow symbols is only implied visually.",
